# Use Case

`use_cases.py` holds the component's Use Cases — I/O entries (HTTP, CLI, bot, job)
written as readable scenarios. A Use Case is thin: it calls objects whose
implementation is already hidden — Entities, Repositories, adapters, and shared
helpers — and the detailed low-level implementation itself never lives at this
layer.

## Shape

A Use Case reads top-to-bottom like the scenario it implements: load Entities,
call their methods, call collaborators, save, return. Names are business verbs; the
body fits on one screen.

Resolve dependencies at the point of use. Bind one to a local variable only when
the scenario uses it more than once.
Endpoints, handlers, and CLI commands call use cases; other components do not.

## What a Use Case owns

- application policy: scenario authorization, idempotency, retry policy,
  branching, and the order of external calls;
- the transaction boundary around writes that must commit atomically;
- input checks — inline, or in a shared validator;
- application-level outcomes the caller must distinguish.

## What never lives here

Low-level implementation here is a misplacement, not a style choice. Move it into
the object that owns it and keep only the call:

| Written inside a Use Case | Belongs to |
|---|---|
| SQL / ORM queries, filters, joins, session mechanics | a Repository method |
| URLs, headers, tokens, status-code branching, response parsing | an outbound adapter |
| raw payload / JSON shaping, wire-format mapping | a boundary schema or the owning adapter |
| hand-rolled retry loops, sleeps, backoff arithmetic | a shared retry helper |
| invariant checks, state transitions, domain calculations | Entity methods or domain functions |
| parsing, normalizing, encoding external values | an adapter or domain value type |
| constructing infrastructure clients | the composition root or dependency provider |

Rule of thumb: when a reader has to reconstruct mechanics instead of following the
scenario, the line is in the wrong layer. The fix is a method on the called object —
not a comment explaining the mechanics.
